{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1547512671,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1NDIyMDA3Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3121#issuecomment-454220073"},"message":"\u003e The idea behind this would be to provide a simple way for our users to implement the actual loading of data using the library or method that they prefer without having to worry too much about the plumbing.\n\ncould you demonstrate with an ideal prototypical invocation (bash or python) you expect your users to do?\n\n\u003e ... I imagine however that this would be highly dependant on the features offered by git-annex as it would need to have an interface allowing to specify which bytes to download.\n\nDataLad (as well as git/git-annex) operates on files as the smallest partition. (well -- there is chunking in git-annex for special remotes but I do not think it would relate here).  The only \"exception\" to it I just discovered is [git-annex-inprogress](https://git-annex.branchable.com/git-annex-inprogress/) but I also do not think it is a solution for your case since you would be able to fetch/stream only from the beginning of the file.  Given your particular demand and setup, I guess it should be relatively easy to provide a solution on top of git-annex or datalad to just query them for the locations (ssh or urls) of the corresponding file and then do your remote access for it for the corresponding byte range.  Alternative could be - talk to/hire @joeyh and see if he would be interested to extend git-annex to support your usecase.","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1547611788,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDIwNzYzOTYyNTk="},"added":["severity-wishlist"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"d0245b1b7fca4b5f4c849e556c4539e8f7efe90a"},"timestamp":1547650534,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1NDgwODAyMg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3121#issuecomment-454808022"},"message":"Thank you for the quick response. I just got a change in my schedule and will have to respond next Monday.\n\nSorry for the delay","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"d0245b1b7fca4b5f4c849e556c4539e8f7efe90a"},"timestamp":1548109974,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1NjIxNTM4NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3121#issuecomment-456215384"},"message":"Here a example of usage:\n```python\nfrom datalad import api\nfrom datalad_plugin import Loader\n\ndataset = api.Dataset(path)\n\ndataloader = Loader(dataset,\n                    batch_size=32,\n                    # kwargs would be transfered to an internal data loader like\n                    # a h5py file if data is in an hdf5.\n                    dataloader_kwargs={shuffle=True,\n                                       seed=1234,\n                                       transform=transform1})\n\n# Load all data in memory. This is useful but not required in most of our\n# researchers use cases. Depending on the data container, this could the container\n# itself like a h5py file if data is in an hdf5.\nentire_dataset = dataloader.load()\n# do something with the elements of the dataset.\nprint(len(entire_dataset))\n\n# Load data using batches. This is almost always the way data is loaded in our\n# researchers use cases.\nfor batch in dataloader.load_batch():\n    # do something with the batch of elements.\n    print(len(batch))\n\n# Load all data contained in a partition (in memory should be good enough for\n# most cases). Partitions need to be define in one way or another else the\n# dataset will be considered to have a single partition. An example would be to\n# have data separated in directories. For example, if you have cats and dogs in\n# separated directories, it would then be easy to pull all cats for analysis.\n# This is useful but not required in most of our researchers use cases.\nfor element in dataloader.load_partition(index=0):\n    # do something with the partition's data.\n    print(element)\n```\n\nA bit more practical example with `load_batch`\n```python\ndef train(model, train_loader):\n    for batch in train_loader.load_batch():\n        # train the model\n        print(len(batch))\n\ndef validation(model, valid_loader):\n    for batch in valid_loader.load_batch():\n        # compute accuracy\n        print(len(batch))\n\n    return accuracy\n\ndataloader_kwargs = {shuffle=True, seed=1234, transform=transform1}\n\nmodel = ...\n\ntrain_set = api.Dataset(path_train)\nvalid_set = api.Dataset(path_valid)\n\ntrain_loader = Loader(train_set, batch_size=32, dataloader_kwargs=dataloader_kwargs)\nvalid_loader = Loader(valid_set, batch_size=32, dataloader_kwargs=dataloader_kwargs)\n\nfor i in range(10):\n    train(model, train_loader)\n    accuracy = validation(model, valid_loader)\n```\n\nThis is pretty much what is proposed by the [intake](https://github.com/ContinuumIO/intake) project in a subsection of its [`DataSource` interface](https://github.com/ContinuumIO/intake/blob/80f7178dc7331221611cc774f968b7d38e41d1bb/intake/source/base.py#L198).\n\nIt's good to know that a \"streaming\" feature would fit in the DataLad landscape. I will follow your suggestion and first contact @joeyh.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1548130249,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1NjI2NTE3NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3121#issuecomment-456265175"},"message":"what is a batch_size there - # of bytes or files?  if files -- could be done outside of git-annex","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1548148418,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1NjMyNTA5OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3121#issuecomment-456325099"},"message":"FTR: While `git-annex-inprogress` might be insufficient for that use case (not clear to me), I think it's a very interesting feature regardless. We might want to integrate it for basic streaming even if it turns out, that we can't really address the actual issue at hand.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"d0245b1b7fca4b5f4c849e556c4539e8f7efe90a"},"timestamp":1548194505,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1NjU4MDI5Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3121#issuecomment-456580292"},"message":"`batch_size=32` represents the number of entries of the dataset to load in a single batch. So if the dataset is represented with multiple files, it could indeed be the number of files but it would not if the dataset is contained in a single file, like a .csv or an hdf5.\n\nA per file or per bytes \"streaming\" feature given by a plugin interface on top of DataLad would be useful but the customization of using it or not and how would be in the plugin implementation as not all datasets would be compatible with that feature. For example, some of the datasets could be compressed in a single container and not all compression formats support random access to its content.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1578612038,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU3MjgwMTk5OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3121#issuecomment-572801998"},"message":"now related: https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/4003","files":null}]}